#!/bin/bash
# Shadows the real gamescope binary via PATH (see
# gamescope-session.service.d/override.conf's PATH= override) --
# /usr/lib/steamos/gamescope-session's own `exec gamescope \ ...` line is
# hardcoded with no env-var-driven way to append extra flags, so this is
# the only way to inject --expose-wayland without patching that packaged
# script directly (which would get overwritten on the next
# gamescope-session-cachyos update).
#
# --expose-wayland: confirmed via the ArchWiki Gamescope page -- "Gamescope
# does not support Wayland clients by default." Without this flag, a game
# launched with PROTON_ENABLE_WAYLAND=1 has nowhere to actually connect --
# its Wayland surface silently never gets created, which is exactly why
# that experiment hung forever on Steam's "waiting for game" spinner rather
# than showing an error. Harmless to keep even with PROTON_ENABLE_WAYLAND
# unset -- XWayland-hosted games are unaffected either way.
#
# Tried --hdr-enabled --hdr-itm-enable here too (gamescope's own
# compositor-level HDR + inverse-tone-mapping, separate from the per-game
# WSI hooking) -- no improvement, tested both with and without the WSI
# layer enabled alongside it. Photographed proof (two distinct dialogs,
# CreateSwapchainKHR and QueuePresentKHR, both "hooking has failed
# somewhere") confirmed the actual bug is in gamescope's own surface-hook
# registration itself, upstream of anything fixable via flags or env vars
# -- clicking OK on either dialog just continues with a fully unhooked
# swapchain, so gamescope never gets a chance to treat that window's
# buffer as HDR regardless of --hdr-enabled. Reverted; only
# --expose-wayland remains, since that one is still correct/harmless
# regardless of whether native-Wayland mode ever gets used again.
exec /usr/bin/gamescope --expose-wayland "$@"
